{0 Template context}

A vault snapshot serialized to JSON, for a Jinja template to compute a document
from. This is the third client of the index, alongside the LSP and the command
line: where those answer questions about one note at a time, this one hands the
whole snapshot to a template engine so a build system can generate notes —
a table of contents over a subtree, an index filtered by tag, a list of
recently updated notes.

Implementation: {!Oystermark.Context}.

{1 The boundary}

[oyster context] writes JSON to stdout and does nothing else. Rendering belongs
to a Jinja engine invoked by the build system.

Splitting there keeps two properties. The library gains no runtime dependency on
a template binary, so a build that never renders never needs one. And the
context is an ordinary value: it can be diffed, committed, and expect-tested,
as every other vault query is.

{v
oyster context VAULT [-compact]
v}

The queried vault is loaded with embed expansion skipped. An expanded embed
repeats another note's syntax inside its host, which would report links the
author never wrote there.

{1 Namespace}

{2 Top level}

{ul
 {- [vault] — [root], [note_count], [asset_count], [link_count],
    [orphan_count], [unresolved_count].}
 {- [notes] — every note, in ascending canonical path order.}
 {- [notes_by_path] — the same notes keyed by path, for following a link or a
    backlink to its destination.}
 {- [assets] — non-Markdown files: [path], [dir], [segments], [name], [stem].}
 {- [tags] — tag to the paths carrying it, sorted by tag.}
 {- [orphans] — paths no other note references.}
 {- [unresolved] — every link that does not resolve: [source], [target],
    [fragment], [kind], [reason], [line].}
}

{2 A note}

{ul
 {- [path], [dir], [name], [stem] — [dir] is [""] at the vault root, not ["."],
    so a template can compare it against an authored prefix directly.}
 {- [segments] — [dir] split into components, for grouping by subtree.}
 {- [title] — see {!Oystermark.Vault.Index.Entry.title}.}
 {- [tags] — see {!Oystermark.Vault.Index.Entry.tags}.}
 {- [frontmatter] — the parsed YAML as JSON, so a template can read keys this
    library has no opinion about. [{}] when the note has no frontmatter, or
    when its frontmatter is not a mapping and so has no keys to index.}
 {- [created], [modified] — [YYYY-MM-DD] or [null]. See {!dates}.}
 {- [headings] — [text], [level], [slug], [line]. The [slug] is the identifier
    the parser assigned, so a link built from it resolves.}
 {- [links] — outgoing: [kind] ([link] or [embed]), [target], [fragment],
    [resolved], [broken], [reason], [line]. [broken] restates [resolved]'s
    nullity as a boolean, because [{% if link.broken %}] reads better than a
    null test.}
 {- [backlinks] — incoming: [source], [title], [kind], [line]. [title] is the
    source note's, so a backlink list needs no second lookup. Links aimed at an
    anchor the note owns count as backlinks to the note.}
 {- [link_count], [backlink_count], [is_orphan].}
}

{2:dates Dates}

[created] is authored, not stat'd: it reads the frontmatter [created] key, then
[date]. OCaml's [Unix.stat] exposes no [st_birthtime], and a creation time that
survives neither a [git] checkout nor a file copy would be a poor sort key
regardless.

[modified] prefers an authored frontmatter [updated] key and otherwise reports
the file's mtime, which a checkout or a formatting pass can bump without the
note's content meaningfully changing.

Both are ISO-8601 strings rather than structured values so a template can sort
on them directly: [YYYY-MM-DD] orders lexicographically exactly as it orders
chronologically.

{1 Cost}

Serializing forces the snapshot's reverse index once, so the whole context is
[O(notes + links)] rather than quadratic in the number of notes. This is why
every note can carry its backlinks eagerly: a template may ask each note how
many notes point at it without the cost compounding.

{1 Build integration}

{v
(rule
 (targets index.md)
 (deps
  (source_tree vault)
  index.md.jinja)
 (action
  (with-stdout-to
   index.md
   (pipe-stdout
    (run %{bin:oyster} context vault)
    (run minijinja-cli --format=json index.md.jinja -)))))
v}

The [source_tree] dependency is coarse on purpose: a template that queries the
whole vault depends on every note in it, so touching any note rebuilds every
generated page. Fine-grained dependencies would require knowing
which notes a given template actually read, which the template engine does not
report.

A worked setup — the tree, several templates, and the rules that build them —
is in {!page-"template-context-example"}.

{1 Example}

Group every note by directory, then list active OCaml projects newest first:

{v
# Vault Index

{{ vault.note_count }} notes, {{ vault.unresolved_count }} unresolved.

{% for dir, group in notes | groupby("dir") %}
## {{ dir if dir else "(root)" }}
{% for n in group %}
- [{{ n.title }}]({{ n.path }}){% if n.backlink_count %} ({{ n.backlink_count }} backlinks){% endif %}
{% endfor %}
{% endfor %}

## Newest OCaml projects

{% for n in notes | selectattr("created") | sort(attribute="created", reverse=true) %}
{% if "ocaml" in n.tags %}- {{ n.created }} — [{{ n.title }}]({{ n.path }})
{% endif %}{% endfor %}
v}
